        {
          "title": "RUlE 2 / dEFinitions FR-41",
          "content": "",
          "start_page": 44,
          "id": "mens_football-rule-176",
          "snippet": ""
        },
        {
          "title": "SECTION 23. SNAPPING THE BALL",
          "content": "",
          "start_page": 44,
          "id": "mens_football-rule-177",
          "snippet": ""
        },
        {
          "title": "ARTICLE 1\b a\b Legally snapping the ball (a snap) is handing or passing it",
          "content": "backward from its position on the ground with a quick and continuous motion\nof the hand or hands, the ball actually leaving the hand or hands in this motion\n(Rule 4-1-4)\b\nb\b The snap starts when the ball is moved legally and ends when the ball\nleaves the snapper’s hands; the ball then becomes alive (Rule 4-1-1, A\bR\b\n2-23-1-I-II, A\bR\b 7-1-5-I-II)\b\nc\b If, during any backward motion of a legal snap, the ball slips from the\nsnapper’s hand, it becomes a backward pass and is in play (Rule 4-1-1)\b\nd\b While resting on the ground and before the snap, the long axis of the ball\nmust be at right angles to the scrimmage line (Rule 7-1-3)\b\ne\b Unless moved in a backward direction, the movement of the ball does not\nstart a legal snap\b It is not a legal snap if the ball is first moved forward or\nlifted\b\nf\b If the ball is touched by Team B during a legal snap, the ball remains dead\nand Team B is penalized\b If the ball is touched by Team B during an illegal\nsnap, the ball remains dead and Team A is penalized (A\bR\b 7-1-5-I-II)\b\ng\b The snap need not be between the snapper’s legs; but to be legal, it must\nbe a quick and continuous backward motion\b\nh\b The ball must be snapped on or between the hash marks\b",
          "start_page": 44,
