        {
          "title": "APPENDIX G / VIDEO REVIEw 173",
          "content": "interference in relation to runner’s lane; retire runner interference; a\nbatted ball hitting a baserunner; and batter-runner interference in relation\nto a batted ball hitting the batter-runner in the box (foul ball) or out of\nthe box (out)�\n4� Obstruction�\n5� Deciding if malicious/flagrant contact occurred� Umpires may initiate this\nreview without requiring a coach’s challenge at any point in the game to ensure\nstudent-athlete safety�\n6� Timing plays (deciding whether a third out is made before the lead base runner\ntouches home plate)�\n7� Force/Tag Play Calls: Plays involving all runners acquiring the base before the\ndefensive player’s attempt to put the runner out at any base�\n8� Blocked or dead ball/Placement of Runners: Deciding whether a ball not ruled\nblocked should be ruled blocked, and the proper placement of runners (per the\nrules/case book) after any blocked or dead ball call�\n9� A catch/no catch�\na� Any catch or no catch in the outfield (beyond the nearest infielder) or in\nfoul territory is reviewable at any time�\nb� A “no catch” ruling in the infield can be changed to “catch” only if it\nresults in the third out with a runner(s) on base, or any time with a batter-\nrunner only and no base runners�\nc� A called “catch” on a fair ball in the infield is not reviewable at any time\n10� Runners leaving the base prior to the touch on a fly ball (tagging up), runners\nmissing a base and runners leaving early on a pitch�\na� The crew chief may not initiate a review of runners leaving early on a\npitch� This review is only allowed by a coach's challenge�\nb� If leaving early is not called on the field and a runner successfully advances\na base on the play, this is reviewable with a coach’s challenge except in the\nfollowing situations: Out of the park home run, hit by pitch, ground rule\ndouble, foul ball, lead off, or blocked ball base award on a pitch, a walk\nor catcher obstruction�\n11� Base running situations:\na� A baserunner passes a preceding runner before that runner is put out�\nException: Rule 12�4�3�\nb� If a batter runner after overrunning first base makes an attempt toward\nsecond base�\n12� Recordkeeping� Umpires may utilize video review at any time during the game\nfor recordkeeping items such as the count, number of outs, what player was\nat bat, substitutions, etc�\nCriteria for using video review:\n1� All equipment should be tested by appropriate personnel before each game�\n2� A ruling on the field will only be changed if there is indisputable video evidence\nto reverse the call� Absent that evidence, the original ruling on the field will\nstand�\n\n[PAGE 177]\n174 APPENDIX G / VIDEO REVIEw\n3� Head coaches have 30 seconds to challenge a play� It must occur before the next\npitch and before umpires leave the field of play�\n4� The video review may be conducted on-site by the field umpires or by an off-\nfield official at a centralized video review location� If being conducted by the\non-field umpires the calling umpire shall not be involved in the review and shall\nremain on the field�\n5� If video review is conducted on-site by the field umpires, the video review area\nshall not require an umpire to view the video within any team area� Umpires\nmay walk through dugouts to get to the reviewing location� At least one umpire\nmust remain on the field�\n6� During the video review, the defensive team shall remain on the field, but may\nhuddle or warmup if desired� Offensive base runners, current batter and the on\ndeck batter also shall remain on the field, but may leave their current position�\nAll players and non-coaching team personnel must remain in their respective\ndugouts� Neither team will be charged with a conference in these situations, but\nteams must be ready to play when the umpires return from the review� If the\ndefensive team is not ready, a ball will be awarded to the batter� If the offensive\nteam delays in any manner, a strike will be assessed to the batter�\n7� Once the review is completed, the crew chief will communicate the ruling\nto both head coaches and the official scorer using the following criteria� This\nshould also be communicated to the broadcasting booth, if applicable, via the\numpire who has a microphone�\na� Ruling on the field is confirmed (clear video evidence call on field was\ncorrect);\nb� Ruling on the field stands (no clear indisputable video evidence to\nconfirm or change the call); or\nc� Ruling on the field is changed (clear video evidence that the call on the\nfield was incorrect)�\n8� If the reversing of a call results in the need for decisions on the placement of base\nrunners, the crew chief shall use their best judgment and/or the appropriate rule\nto determine where to place the runners as if the call had been made correctly�\n9� All reviewable elements of a play will be reviewed at the base or plate where the\noriginal call occurred� If a coach would like to challenge an additional call not at\nthe base or plate of the original challenge, a second challenge would be needed\nto review that call� The only exception to this is when there is a routine, normal\nor signature double play� In this case both elements of this play can be reviewed\nwith a single challenge�\n10� Multiple challenges on the same play are handled in the order they are\nchallenged, and every challenge counts toward the team’s total - even if the\nresults of one cancels another�\n11� The final decision may not be argued by either coach� A coach who argues the\nfinal decision shall be immediately ejected�\n\n[PAGE 178]",
